<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════
     HEADER
══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="label">Competitive Intelligence · Product Strategy</div>
    <h1>Generative AI Competitive Landscape:<br>User Sentiment &amp; Complaint Analysis Across Top 5 AI Apps</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A data-driven analysis of 41,729 validated Google Play Store reviews across ChatGPT, Claude, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, and Perplexity — Q1 2026</p>
    <div class="report-meta">
      <span><strong>Reviews Analyzed:</strong> 41,729</span>
      <span><strong>Apps Covered:</strong> 5</span>
      <span><strong>Report Date:</strong> April 21, 2026</span>
      <span><strong>Audience:</strong> Product Strategy Team</span>
      <span><strong>Data Source:</strong> US Google Play Store, Early 2026</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

  <!-- TOC -->
  <div class="toc">
    <h3>Table of Contents</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#exec">Executive Overview</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sentiment">Cross-App Sentiment Comparison</a></li>
      <li><a href="#themes">Thematic Complaint Analysis</a></li>
      <li><a href="#divergence">Sentiment-Rating Divergence &amp; Sarcasm Detection</a></li>
      <li><a href="#depth">Review Depth &amp; Emotional Intensity</a></li>
      <li><a href="#recs">Strategic Recommendations</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 1 — EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="exec">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>
      <p>Three key strategic takeaways for the product team — read this before anything else.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- KPI strip -->
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="val" style="color:#0078d4;">4.24</div>
        <div class="lbl">Star Rating Leader</div>
        <div class="sub">Microsoft Copilot</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="val" style="color:#d97706;">0.375</div>
        <div class="lbl">Best Sentiment Score</div>
        <div class="sub">Claude (2nd overall)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="val" style="color:#8b5cf6;">31.3%</div>
        <div class="lbl">Highest 1-Star Rate</div>
        <div class="sub">Perplexity</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="val" style="color:#991b1b;">13.6%</div>
        <div class="lbl">Highest Mismatch Rate</div>
        <div class="sub">Claude</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="val" style="color:#0f172a;">41,729</div>
        <div class="lbl">Reviews Analyzed</div>
        <div class="sub">~8,300 per app</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Three Takeaways -->
    <div class="takeaway">
      <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>Microsoft Copilot dominates satisfaction metrics — but its lead is structural, not earned through AI quality</h4>
        <p>Copilot leads all apps in average star rating (4.24★), positive sentiment polarity (0.487), and 5-star rate (68.2%), while having the lowest 1-star rate (11.3%). However, its complaint profile reveals the fewest Accuracy and Bug complaints partly because it has the highest proportion of "General" reviews (90.3%) — suggesting many users are not pushing it hard enough to encounter failures. Its low engagement thumbs-up scores (3.48) relative to sentiment-rich Perplexity (2.79 with far more criticism) indicate its satisfied users are passive rather than vocal advocates. This is a ceiling risk, not a moat.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>Pricing friction is the #1 strategic vulnerability for Claude and Perplexity — both hemorrhaging vocal users over paywalls</h4>
