    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-header">
        <span class="priority-chip pri-high">P0 — Critical</span>
        <h4>Address Claude's Pricing Perception Before It Compounds</h4>
      </div>
      <p>Claude leads all apps in Pricing complaint rate (8.0%) and its pricing reviews attract the highest community endorsement (6.18 avg thumbs-up) of any non-bug complaint across all five apps. Left unaddressed, this creates a feedback loop: frustrated users post, others upvote, and the negative signal dominates search impressions. Immediate actions: (a) audit in-app paywall messaging for clarity and fairness framing; (b) introduce a visible "free tier features" summary to reduce expectation mismatch; (c) consider a referral credit mechanic to convert pricing critics into advocates. This is a messaging and positioning fix as much as a pricing fix — the data suggests users are not uniformly opposed to paying, but feel surprised or misled.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-header">
        <span class="priority-chip pri-high">P0 — Critical</span>
        <h4>Leverage Claude's Accuracy Advantage as a Core Positioning Signal</h4>
      </div>
      <p>Claude has the lowest Accuracy complaint rate (4.3%) among all five apps — nearly 2.1 percentage points below Perplexity (6.4%). Combined with a negative accuracy sentiment in competitors (Perplexity: −0.180, ChatGPT: −0.176) vs. Claude's mildly positive accuracy sentiment (+0.093), there is measurable evidence that users perceive Claude as more reliable. This should be front-and-center in marketing copy, app store descriptions, and onboarding flows — particularly targeting Perplexity and ChatGPT users who cite hallucination frustrations. The competitive window for this positioning is time-sensitive as rivals iterate.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-header">
        <span class="priority-chip pri-high">P1 — High</span>
        <h4>Resolve the Bug and Stability Issues Driving Claude's 5.5% Bug Complaint Rate</h4>
      </div>
      <p>Claude's Bug complaint rate (5.5%) is the highest among all apps and tied with Google Gemini, despite Claude's generally stronger sentiment profile. Bug reviews for Claude average 2.55 thumbs-up — indicating community resonance. The product and engineering teams should prioritize a stability sprint targeting the most commonly referenced crash and performance regression patterns in these reviews. Bug complaints, when fixed and communicated via app store changelogs, have a documented positive effect on rating recovery. Addressing this will also reduce the mismatch rate (currently 13.6%) driven partly by users who liked the app but are frustrated by instability.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-header">
        <span class="priority-chip pri-medium">P2 — Medium</span>
        <h4>Use Perplexity's Bug Amplification as a Competitive Opportunity</h4>
      </div>
      <p>Perplexity's bug reviews attract 27.5 avg thumbs-up per review — an outlier that signals an engaged power-user base actively experiencing and documenting crashes. These users are likely also Claude's addressable market. A targeted acquisition campaign emphasizing Claude's lower bug complaint rate (2.1× lower) and stability — with specific testimonials about reliability — could convert Perplexity defectors. Monitor Perplexity's app store reviews for new high-thumbs-up bug reports as early indicators of their release quality.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-header">
        <span class="priority-chip pri-medium">P2 — Medium</span>
        <h4>Invest in Review Sentiment Monitoring Infrastructure</h4>
      </div>
      <p>Across all five apps, 13.6% of Claude's reviews exhibit sentiment-rating mismatch — the highest rate measured. Most mismatches (66%) follow a "low-star, positive text" pattern where users write warmly but rate poorly due to a specific friction point (often pricing or a single bug). This means star rating alone underestimates product affinity. A real-time VADER-based review monitoring system that flags mismatch reviews would allow the support team to proactively reach these ambivalent users — who are showing intent to stay engaged — and convert them before churn. The 76.4% zero-thumbs-up rate confirms that most review signals go unvalidated; automated clustering of high-mismatch reviews offers an untapped retention signal.</p>
    </div>

